[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Graph Traversal: Breadth-First Search (BFS) and Depth-First Search (DFS)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Graph traversal is the process of visiting (checking and/or updating) each vertex in a graph. Such traversals are classified by the order in which the vertices are visited. Two common graph traversal algorithms are Breadth-First Search (BFS) and Depth-First Search (DFS)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Breadth-First Search (BFS)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"BFS explores all the neighbor nodes at the present depth prior to moving on to the nodes at the next depth level. It uses a queue data structure to remember which vertex to visit next."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Start at the root (or any arbitrary node) and add it to the queue."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"While the queue is not empty:"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dequeue a vertex from the queue."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mark the dequeued vertex as visited."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enqueue all unvisited neighbors of the dequeued vertex."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"def bfs(graph, start_node):
    visited = set()
    queue = [start_node]
    visited.add(start_node)

    while queue:
        vertex = queue.pop(0) # Dequeue
        print(vertex, end=' ')

        for neighbor in graph[vertex]:
            if neighbor not in visited:
                visited.add(neighbor)
                queue.append(neighbor) # Enqueue"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Depth-First Search (DFS)"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"DFS explores as far as possible along each branch before backtracking. It uses a stack data structure (implicitly via recursion or explicitly) to remember which vertex to visit next when a dead end is reached."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Start at the root (or any arbitrary node)."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mark the current vertex as visited."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"For each unvisited neighbor of the current vertex:"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Recursively call DFS on the neighbor."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"def dfs(graph, vertex, visited):
    visited.add(vertex)
    print(vertex, end=' ')

    for neighbor in graph[vertex]:
        if neighbor not in visited:
            dfs(graph, neighbor, visited)

def dfs_wrapper(graph, start_node):
    visited = set()
    dfs(graph, start_node, visited)"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"BFS uses a queue, while DFS uses a stack (implicitly or explicitly)."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"BFS explores neighbors, then their neighbors, and so on. DFS explores as far as possible along each branch before backtracking."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"BFS is often used to find the shortest path in an unweighted graph. DFS can be used to detect cycles in a graph."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Example Graph Representation"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following example shows a graph represented as an adjacency list in Python.  This representation will work with the code examples above."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"graph = {
    'A': ['B', 'C'],
    'B': ['D', 'E'],
    'C': ['F'],
    'D': [],
    'E': ['F'],
    'F': []
}"}]
